## 3–5 Reusable Findings

1. Defines RegTech as "the use of technology, particularly information technology, in the context of regulatory monitoring, reporting, and compliance" — the canonical definition cited across the corpus (feeds S5).
2. Identifies a paradigm shift from "KYC" (Know Your Customer) to "KYD" (Know Your Data) as the conceptual reorientation RegTech demands — directly relevant to data-governance framing of DPDP/PMLA tensions (feeds S5/S2).
3. Frames RegTech as encompassing three distinct market participant groups — financial institutions (most advanced, cost-driven), regulators (supervisory-capacity-driven), and policymakers/infrastructure-builders (nascent) — useful typology for classifying corpus nodes by RegTech maturity stage (feeds S5).
4. Documents the post-2008 driver: regulatory fines/settlements rose ~45-fold, pushing RegTech adoption industry-wide before regulator-side adoption (historical baseline for [[Governance_Debt]] timeline) (feeds S2).
5. Argues principal regulatory objectives (financial stability, prudential safety, consumer protection, market integrity) remain constant even as RegTech transforms their *means* of application — useful framing for distinguishing ends vs. means in AI-governance debates (feeds S1).

## Consulting / Teaching Reuse

- The KYC→KYD framing is highly reusable as a one-line explainer for LinkedIn thought-leadership on why DPDP-era data governance is inseparable from AI compliance.
- The three-participant RegTech typology (industry / regulator / policymaker) is a useful diagnostic frame for AEGIS_OS positioning — which "RegTech generation" a prospective client institution sits in.

